Testing Plan:
Our program functions as a simple shell environment that supports both interactive and batch modes.
We tested the shell in both modes (no argument interactive mode and batch mode with a file argument),
and verified that behavior remained consistent across both.
Our shell is capable of handling wildcard expansion, built-in commands, and standard Unix command execution.
We tested file and command handling using the following features:
built-in commands: cd, pwd, which, exit
wildcard expansion: patterns such as foo* and *.xyz
execution of external Unix commands (e.g., echo)
We tested various commands including the following:
which echo
which cd
which doesnotexist
pwd
cd /tmp
pwd
cd
pwd
echo foo*
echo *.xyz
exit
We also tested batch mode execution using a script file:
printf 'which echo\npwd\necho foo*\necho *.xyz\n' > test.sh
./mysh.o test.sh
These tests verified:
correct command resolution
proper directory navigation
correct wildcard expansion behavior
handling of nonexistent commands
consistent execution between interactive and batch modes
clean shell termination using exit
Our shell successfully handles malformed or invalid input without crashing.

to run our shell environment:
make
./mysh.o (interactive mode)
./mysh.o test.sh (batch mode)
use exit to leave interactive mode

